# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, git stores whatever line endings a tool happens to write.
# Several editors and R itself write CRLF on Windows while other tooling writes
# LF, so files flip back and forth and show up as fully-rewritten in `git diff`
# even when nothing changed. Real examples from this repo before this file
# existed: src/Ghana/2_GW_Ghana_data_merge.R showed 933 changed lines of which
# only 64 were real, and _targets.yaml / mn-prediction.Rproj showed as fully
# rewritten with zero content change. That churn buries real edits in review.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF in the repository. Working
# copies are unaffected on Linux/macOS; on Windows, editors handle LF fine and R
# reads it without issue.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly text, so git never guesses.
*.R      text eol=lf
*.Rmd    text eol=lf
*.qmd    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.bib    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.Rproj  text eol=lf

# PowerShell needs CRLF to run reliably on Windows.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# Data files: diff as text, but NEVER translate line endings. These CSVs contain
# embedded CRLF inside quoted fields (e.g. metadata/dhs_survey_characteristics.csv
# has a multi-line quoted value), and normalising would silently rewrite the DATA,
# not just the line endings.
*.csv    -text

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text. Serialised R objects in
# particular are corrupted by line-ending translation.
*.rds    binary
*.RData  binary
*.rdb    binary
*.rdx    binary
*.png    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.docx   binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.pptx   binary
*.tif    binary
*.tiff   binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.shp    binary
*.shx    binary
*.dbf    binary
*.prj    binary
*.sbn    binary
*.sbx    binary
